1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,873
Every device on a network needs an address, just like every house needs one.

2
00:00:05,423 --> 00:00:09,035
Today you learn to read that address: what the four numbers mean,

3
00:00:09,035 --> 00:00:10,647
what slash twenty-four means,

4
00:00:10,647 --> 00:00:12,314
and where the gateway fits in.

5
00:00:12,864 --> 00:00:16,939
You will check them live, in a real terminal, and work out the maths yourself.

6
00:00:17,489 --> 00:00:21,393
After this, a network address will never look like a secret code again.

7
00:00:23,443 --> 00:00:25,064
First, the I P address.

8
00:00:25,614 --> 00:00:29,134
An I P address identifies one device on a network.

9
00:00:29,684 --> 00:00:33,222
I P version four uses four numbers,

10
00:00:33,222 --> 00:00:37,365
each from zero to two hundred fifty-five,

11
00:00:37,365 --> 00:00:39,184
separated by dots.

12
00:00:39,184 --> 00:00:41,610
Like this: 192.168.1.25.

13
00:00:42,160 --> 00:00:46,682
Part of the address names the network. The rest names the device on that network.

14
00:00:47,232 --> 00:00:52,203
Think of a street and a house number. The street is the network. The house is the device.

15
00:00:52,753 --> 00:00:56,230
Two devices on the same street can talk to each other directly.

16
00:00:58,280 --> 00:01:03,593
Let us look at the address of this computer's network card.

17
00:01:03,593 --> 00:01:05,395
It is called enp0s3,

18
00:01:05,395 --> 00:01:09,898
the usual name in a VirtualBox machine like yours.

19
00:01:09,898 --> 00:01:12,509
Type: ip -br addr show enp0s3

20
00:01:12,639 --> 00:01:15,178
The state says U P, so the card is working.

21
00:01:15,728 --> 00:01:22,597
And here is the address: 192.168.1.25, then slash twenty-four.

22
00:01:23,147 --> 00:01:28,139
That slash twenty-four says how much of the address names the network. We decode it soon.

23
00:01:28,689 --> 00:01:33,468
Now the routes. Type: ip route show dev enp0s3

24
00:01:33,598 --> 00:01:42,878
The second line says: the network 192.168.1.0/24 is directly reachable from this card.

25
00:01:43,428 --> 00:01:51,321
The first line says: for everything else, go via 192.168.1.1. That is the gateway.

26
00:01:51,871 --> 00:01:56,821
Your numbers will differ, but the shape is the same: an address, a network, and a gateway.

27
00:01:58,871 --> 00:02:00,812
Not every address works everywhere.

28
00:02:01,362 --> 00:02:06,738
Some ranges are private: reserved for use inside homes, offices and cloud networks.

29
00:02:07,288 --> 00:02:08,385
There are three.

30
00:02:08,385 --> 00:02:09,551
Ten dot anything.

31
00:02:09,551 --> 00:02:14,006
One seventy-two dot sixteen up to one seventy-two dot thirty-one.

32
00:02:14,006 --> 00:02:17,571
And one ninety-two dot one sixty-eight dot anything.

33
00:02:18,121 --> 00:02:20,869
Anyone can use them inside their own network,

34
00:02:20,869 --> 00:02:24,350
so the same private address exists in millions of places.

35
00:02:24,900 --> 00:02:30,020
Everything else is public. A public address is unique, and reachable from the internet.

36
00:02:30,570 --> 00:02:33,039
Your home router has one public address,

37
00:02:33,039 --> 00:02:36,124
and shares it with every private device behind it.

38
00:02:36,124 --> 00:02:36,926
We cover how,

39
00:02:36,926 --> 00:02:38,037
in a later lesson.

40
00:02:38,587 --> 00:02:44,091
Careful: one seventy-two dot thirty-two is public. The private range stops at thirty-one.

41
00:02:46,141 --> 00:02:48,441
Let us ask the computer.

42
00:02:48,441 --> 00:02:52,083
We wrote a small helper called ipwhat,

43
00:02:52,083 --> 00:02:55,820
with Python's standard address library.

44
00:02:55,820 --> 00:02:58,216
Type: ipwhat 192.168.1.25

45
00:02:58,346 --> 00:03:00,095
Private. A home-style address.

46
00:03:00,645 --> 00:03:05,530
Next. Type: ipwhat 172.20.5.9

47
00:03:05,660 --> 00:03:09,372
Also private, because twenty sits between sixteen and thirty-one.

48
00:03:09,922 --> 00:03:14,637
Now a famous one. Type: ipwhat 8.8.8.8

49
00:03:14,767 --> 00:03:17,028
Public. A real address on the internet.

50
00:03:19,078 --> 00:03:23,131
Now the slash. This is called CIDR notation, said like cider.

51
00:03:23,681 --> 00:03:28,417
An address is thirty-two bits. Here is ours in binary, in four groups of eight.

52
00:03:28,967 --> 00:03:31,732
The number after the slash says how many bits,

53
00:03:31,732 --> 00:03:33,054
counted from the left,

54
00:03:33,054 --> 00:03:34,075
name the network.

55
00:03:34,075 --> 00:03:36,839
Slash twenty-four: the first twenty-four bits.

56
00:03:37,389 --> 00:03:42,723
The last eight bits are left for devices. Eight bits give two hundred fifty-six addresses.

57
00:03:43,273 --> 00:03:46,491
Two are reserved: the first names the network itself,

58
00:03:46,491 --> 00:03:48,798
and the last is the broadcast address.

59
00:03:49,348 --> 00:03:52,383
A bigger slash number means a smaller network.

60
00:03:52,383 --> 00:03:54,560
Slash twenty-six leaves six bits,

61
00:03:54,560 --> 00:03:56,143
so sixty-four addresses.

62
00:03:56,143 --> 00:03:58,386
Slash sixteen leaves sixteen bits,

63
00:03:58,386 --> 00:04:01,487
so sixty-five thousand five hundred thirty-six.

64
00:04:02,037 --> 00:04:04,298
A subnet is just one of these slices.

65
00:04:04,848 --> 00:04:13,915
The subnet mask says the same thing in another way: 255.255.255.0 means slash twenty-four.

66
00:04:15,965 --> 00:04:19,988
Let us do the maths with the computer.

67
00:04:19,988 --> 00:04:21,682
A second helper,

68
00:04:21,682 --> 00:04:23,164
called subnet,

69
00:04:23,164 --> 00:04:24,435
lays it out.

70
00:04:24,435 --> 00:04:27,293
Type: subnet 192.168.1.0/24

71
00:04:27,423 --> 00:04:33,460
The network, and the mask: 255.255.255.0.

72
00:04:34,010 --> 00:04:37,322
Devices live from dot one to dot two fifty-four.

73
00:04:37,322 --> 00:04:39,805
Dot two fifty-five is the broadcast.

74
00:04:39,805 --> 00:04:42,565
That makes two hundred fifty-four hosts.

75
00:04:43,115 --> 00:04:46,827
Now cut it smaller. Type: subnet 192.168.1.0/26

76
00:04:46,957 --> 00:04:49,432
The mask now ends in one ninety-two,

77
00:04:49,432 --> 00:04:51,425
and only sixty-two hosts fit,

78
00:04:51,425 --> 00:04:53,144
dot one to dot sixty-two.

79
00:04:53,694 --> 00:04:59,326
And a big one. Type: subnet 10.0.0.0/16

80
00:04:59,456 --> 00:05:07,498
The mask is 255.255.0.0, and sixty-five thousand five hundred thirty-four hosts fit.

81
00:05:08,048 --> 00:05:12,443
Same method every time. A smaller slash number means a bigger network.

82
00:05:14,493 --> 00:05:15,581
Now the gateway.

83
00:05:16,131 --> 00:05:18,244
When your computer sends a packet,

84
00:05:18,244 --> 00:05:21,848
it asks one question: is the destination on my own subnet?

85
00:05:22,398 --> 00:05:25,406
If yes, it sends the packet directly to that device.

86
00:05:25,956 --> 00:05:26,306
If no,

87
00:05:26,306 --> 00:05:28,348
it hands the packet to the gateway.

88
00:05:28,348 --> 00:05:29,456
That is the router,

89
00:05:29,456 --> 00:05:31,439
and it forwards the packet onward.

90
00:05:31,989 --> 00:05:34,094
The gateway must sit on your own subnet,

91
00:05:34,094 --> 00:05:36,725
because it is the one door you can reach directly.

92
00:05:37,275 --> 00:05:40,710
Your computer decides using your address and your slash number.

93
00:05:42,760 --> 00:05:46,296
Let us ask the same question.

94
00:05:46,296 --> 00:05:48,125
A third helper,

95
00:05:48,125 --> 00:05:49,832
called inside,

96
00:05:49,832 --> 00:05:54,465
checks a destination against a subnet.

97
00:05:54,465 --> 00:05:59,464
Type: inside 192.168.1.0/24 192.168.1.200

98
00:05:59,594 --> 00:06:01,791
Inside. So it is sent directly.

99
00:06:02,341 --> 00:06:07,333
Now an internet address. Type: inside 192.168.1.0/24 8.8.8.8

100
00:06:07,463 --> 00:06:09,533
Outside. So it goes to the gateway.

101
00:06:10,083 --> 00:06:16,163
One more thing: names. Type: grep nameserver /etc/resolv.conf

102
00:06:16,293 --> 00:06:19,442
These are the D N S servers this computer asks.

103
00:06:19,442 --> 00:06:21,252
They are I P addresses too.

104
00:06:21,252 --> 00:06:22,056
Public ones,

105
00:06:22,056 --> 00:06:22,927
in this case.

106
00:06:24,977 --> 00:06:28,006
Every working network connection needs four settings.

107
00:06:28,556 --> 00:06:31,074
One: an I P address, which says who you are.

108
00:06:31,624 --> 00:06:35,400
Two: a subnet mask, or slash number, which says who is local.

109
00:06:35,950 --> 00:06:38,211
Three: a gateway, which is the way out.

110
00:06:38,761 --> 00:06:42,388
Four: a D N S server, which turns names into addresses.

111
00:06:42,938 --> 00:06:47,546
Check these four, in this order, and most basic network problems become simple.

112
00:06:49,596 --> 00:06:51,239
Match the symptom to the setting.

113
00:06:51,789 --> 00:06:55,728
An address starting 169.254 means that D H C P,

114
00:06:55,728 --> 00:06:58,830
the service that hands out addresses,

115
00:06:58,830 --> 00:07:00,087
did not answer.

116
00:07:00,637 --> 00:07:04,648
Network is unreachable: there is no route, often a missing gateway.

117
00:07:05,198 --> 00:07:09,038
Local devices work but the internet does not: check the gateway.

118
00:07:09,588 --> 00:07:13,172
An address works but a name fails: check the D N S server.

119
00:07:13,722 --> 00:07:16,730
Random drops: two devices may share one address.

120
00:07:18,780 --> 00:07:20,636
Watch out for four common myths.

121
00:07:21,186 --> 00:07:23,525
Myth one: private addresses are secure.

122
00:07:23,525 --> 00:07:25,983
They are only not routed on the internet.

123
00:07:25,983 --> 00:07:27,842
That is not a security feature.

124
00:07:28,392 --> 00:07:31,328
Myth two: every network is slash twenty-four.

125
00:07:31,328 --> 00:07:35,048
Any size from slash zero to slash thirty-two is possible.

126
00:07:35,598 --> 00:07:39,267
Myth three: all two hundred fifty-six addresses are usable.

127
00:07:39,267 --> 00:07:40,324
Two are reserved,

128
00:07:40,324 --> 00:07:42,190
so two hundred fifty-four are.

129
00:07:42,740 --> 00:07:47,497
Myth four: the gateway can be any device. It must be a router on your own subnet.

130
00:07:48,047 --> 00:07:49,839
Know the real size, then count.

131
00:07:51,889 --> 00:07:54,214
Here is how you check your own understanding.

132
00:07:54,764 --> 00:07:58,476
Explain the network part and the host part of an I P address.

133
00:07:59,026 --> 00:08:01,394
Say whether an address is private or public.

134
00:08:01,944 --> 00:08:04,291
Read slash notation and name the mask.

135
00:08:04,841 --> 00:08:06,974
Work out how many hosts fit in a subnet.

136
00:08:07,524 --> 00:08:09,466
Say when traffic goes to the gateway.

137
00:08:10,016 --> 00:08:12,640
Name the four settings a working network needs.

138
00:08:13,190 --> 00:08:14,811
To pass, you need all six.

139
00:08:16,861 --> 00:08:19,144
Your turn, in the virtual machine you built.

140
00:08:19,694 --> 00:08:24,643
One: run ip -br addr, and find your own address and slash number.

141
00:08:25,193 --> 00:08:26,861
Two: run ip route,

142
00:08:26,861 --> 00:08:28,899
and find your gateway.

143
00:08:28,899 --> 00:08:37,609
With the default VirtualBox network you will probably see 10.0.2.15 and a gateway of 10.0.2.2.

144
00:08:38,159 --> 00:08:42,767
Three: decide whether your address is private or public, using the three ranges.

145
00:08:43,317 --> 00:08:46,240
Four: work out how many hosts your slash number allows.

146
00:08:46,790 --> 00:08:50,122
Five: read your D N S server with grep nameserver.

147
00:08:50,122 --> 00:08:52,654
On Ubuntu it may show a local address,

148
00:08:52,654 --> 00:08:53,787
and that is fine.

149
00:08:54,337 --> 00:08:57,644
Mark yourself on the rubric. Pause the video, and do it now.

150
00:09:13,194 --> 00:09:14,069
Quick check.

151
00:09:14,619 --> 00:09:17,755
What do the network part and the host part of an address mean?

152
00:09:18,305 --> 00:09:20,054
Which three ranges are private?

153
00:09:20,604 --> 00:09:23,697
What does slash twenty-six mean, and how many hosts fit?

154
00:09:24,247 --> 00:09:26,103
When does a computer use the gateway?

155
00:09:26,653 --> 00:09:30,749
Objective covered: MC03-O01.

156
00:09:31,299 --> 00:09:33,369
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

157
00:09:43,919 --> 00:09:46,564
Next: T C P, U D P, and ports.

158
00:09:47,114 --> 00:09:48,991
Subscribe, so you do not miss it.
